<header><p class="st-kicker">emirate · West Africa</p><h1 class="st-title">Kano Emirate</h1><p class="st-meta">1805 CE – 1903 CE · capital Kano</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">The richest emirate of the Sokoto Caliphate, centred on the walled city of Kano, the great manufacturing and trading hub of the central Sudan.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>Between 1805 and 1807 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fula">Fulani</a> jihadists loyal to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/usman-dan-fodio">Usman dan Fodio</a> overthrew the last <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hausa">Hausa</a> ruler of the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/kingdom-of-kano">Kingdom of Kano</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/muhammad-alwali-ii">Muhammad Alwali</a>. The scholar <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sulaimanu-of-kano">Sulaimanu</a> became the first emir, and in 1819 he was succeeded by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ibrahim-dabo">Ibrahim Dabo</a>, whose descendants, the Dabo dynasty, have provided <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kano">Kano's</a> <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/emir">emirs</a> ever since.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>The emir governed through a council of titled officials, many inherited from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hausa">Hausa</a> <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kano">Kano</a>, with district heads collecting taxes from farms and crafts. Kano produced <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/indigo">indigo</a>-dyed cloth and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/leather">leather goods</a> sold across the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sahara">Sahara</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sudan">Sudan</a>, and its market drew caravans from <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/county-of-tripoli">Tripoli</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bornu">Bornu</a> and the forest. The explorer <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/heinrich-barth">Heinrich Barth</a> described it in 1851 as the commercial centre of the region.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>A civil war over the succession in 1893-1895 weakened the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/emirate">emirate</a>. British forces took <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kano">Kano</a> in February 1903, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/emir">Emir</a> <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aliyu-babba">Aliyu Babba</a> was deposed. Kano became the centrepiece of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/indirect-rule">indirect rule</a> in <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/protectorate-of-northern-nigeria">Northern Nigeria</a>, and the emirate continues today.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/kano-emirate.jpg" alt="Kano Emirate" width="320" height="192"><figcaption>Image: Bohr · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Unconfirmed_Kano_Flag.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1805 CE – 1903 CE</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a> – <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>emirate</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>theocratic</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Kano</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Sulaimanu, Ibrahim Dabo, Aliyu Babba</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Sunni Islam</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Hausa, Fulfulde</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/nigeria">Nigeria</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Emirate of Kano</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1854&amp;focus=kano-emirate" aria-label="Kano Emirate on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4914 1730 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Kano Emirate lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M5234 1986L5236 1986M5234 1986L5144 1947M5234 1986L5170 2068"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M5236 1986h0M5144 1947h0M5170 2068h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5234 1986h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5234 1986h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks its recorded location. <a href="/atlas?year=1854&amp;focus=kano-emirate">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>
